#  ---- D11 (round 5): the CLASS CLOSURE for the stored-bit filtering class -------------------
#  WHY A CLASS CLOSURE RATHER THAN TWO MORE ROWS.  Table 4 came back clean and the ask-again then
#  found filtering escapes at MASK0 and BASE1 -- the SECOND consecutive pass to find one breach of
#  one class, which is the carrier's trigger to stop buying cells one at a time and enumerate the
#  whole space in ONE diff.  The space is {MASK, BASE} x {window 0, window 1} x {reserved-bit
#  filtering, field-source selection, zero acceptance, invalid-field rejection}, and the cells that
#  no existing row held are R1-R6 below.
#
#  THE INPUTS ARE THE 64-BIT COMPLEMENTS OF THE STORE MASKS, and that is the whole trick:
#      ~0xfff00000  == 0xffffffff000fffff   (MASK: architected bits 20-31)
#      ~0x3fff00000 == 0xfffffffc000fffff   (BASE: W_BASE 20-31, SG 32, WEN 33)
#  so EVERY reserved bit is set and EVERY architected bit is clear.  A store mask widened by even
#  one bit therefore reads back that bit instead of zero, and a mask NARROWED by one bit is caught
#  at the SEED readback, whose value sets every architected bit.  One pair of writes per register
#  covers narrowing and widening in both directions.
#
#  AND THE FIELD-SOURCE CELL FALLS OUT OF THE SAME INPUT.  With the shipped normalisation
#  f = (idata & 0xfff00000) >> 20 the complement gives f = 0, f & (f+1) = 0, LEGAL, stored as 0.
#  Validate the RAW input instead and f = 0xffffffff000, f & (f+1) = 0xffffffff000 != 0, DECLINED --
#  so the seed value is retained and the row reds.  Reserved input bits 32-63 become deciding field
#  bits 12-43 under raw validation, which is why the complement separates the two readings and a
#  merely-large legal value would not.
#
#  Each row scores the PAIR (seed readback, final readback): the seed is not a free-standing row
#  because a rule that rejects the seed and a rule that rejects the final write are different
#  mutants, and one row that names both states separates them without inflating the denominator.
S_MASK = 0xffffffff000fffff        # ~0xfff00000
S_BASE = 0xfffffffc000fffff        # ~0x3fff00000
txt, alive, vals = seq([k0(IOC_W_MASK0), 0xfff00000, S_MASK,      # slots 0,1,2
                        k0(IOC_W_MASK1), 0x700000, 0x200000,      # slots 3,4,5
                        k0(IOC_W_BASE0), 0x1fff00000, S_BASE,     # slots 6,7,8
                        k0(IOC_W_BASE1)],                         # slot 9
                       [[("st", 0, 1), ("ld", 0, 0)],             # R1 seed:  MASK0 all-ones field
                        [("st", 0, 2), ("ld", 0, 0)],             # R1 final: MASK0 complement
                        [("st", 3, 1), ("ld", 3, 3)],             # R2 seed:  MASK1 all-ones field
                        [("st", 3, 2), ("ld", 3, 3)],             # R2 final: MASK1 complement
                        [("st", 3, 4), ("ld", 3, 3)],             # R3 seed:  MASK1 field 7
                        [("st", 3, 5), ("ld", 3, 3)],             # R3 final: MASK1 field 2 (illegal)
                        [("st", 6, 7), ("ld", 6, 6)],             # R4 seed:  BASE0 W_BASE|SG
                        [("st", 6, 8), ("ld", 6, 6)],             # R4 final: BASE0 complement
                        [("st", 9, 7), ("ld", 9, 9)],             # R5 seed:  BASE1 W_BASE|SG
                        [("st", 9, 8), ("ld", 9, 9)]])            # R5 final: BASE1 complement
def pair(i):
    return (nth(vals, i) + "/" + nth(vals, i + 1)) if alive else "dead"

row("wmask0 filter", pair(0), "0xfff00000/0x0",
    "MASK0: the all-ones field stores whole, then its 64-bit COMPLEMENT stores ZERO.  Reds on a "
    "store mask widened by any reserved bit (reads that bit), narrowed by any architected bit "
    "(the seed loses it), or validating the RAW idata instead of the normalised field (f becomes "
    "0xffffffff000, declines, and the seed is retained)")
row("wmask1 filter-zero", pair(2), "0xfff00000/0x0",
    "the same pair at MASK1 -- the sibling window, whose filtering had no row at all.  It also "
    "carries the NORMALISED-ZERO acceptance cell: f == 0 after masking is legal (HRM Table 6-6's "
    "1 MB encoding), so a rule excluding zero keeps 0xfff00000 here")
row("wmask1 f2", pair(4), "0x700000/0x700000",
    "MASK1 invalid-field rejection at FIELD 2, which `wmask1 declined' does not drive (it uses 5 "
    "and 9): 2 & 3 == 2, so bit 1 of the predicate result is what separates `== 0' from `<= 2'.  "
    "A predicate relaxed to `<= 2' stores 0x200000 and reds this row")
row("wbase0 filter", pair(6), "0x1fff00000/0x0",
    "BASE0: W_BASE|SG stores whole (SG at bit 32 and every W_BASE bit), then the complement stores "
    "ZERO.  Bits 0-19 and 34-63 are set in that write, so any reserved bit added to the store mask "
    "reads back")
row("wbase1 filter", pair(8), "0x1fff00000/0x0",
    "the same pair at BASE1.  M47 -- the store mask extended to bit 34 -- retains exactly "
    "0x400000000 here, which is the escape the ask-again found and which no BASE1 row could see")
row("wen silent both", announces(txt, WEN) if alive else "dead", 0,
    "ZERO WEN announces across a session that writes BOTH windows' BASE registers four times with "
    "WEN clear.  `wen silent' touches BASE0 only, so a `idata & WEN' -> `idata | WEN' edit at the "
    "BASE1 arm alone was unrowed -- a sibling gap, not clause-(e) debt")
evidence(txt)


