    Neither was secretly killed by an overlooked input.

    THE MATRIX IS 2 x 4 -- {window 0, window 1} x {stored-bit filtering, MASK field-source selection, zero
    acceptance, MASK invalid-field rejection} -- and the existing 67 rows already cover five of the eight
    cells (`wmask0 hif', `wmask0 zero', `wbase0 zero', `wmask0 declined'/`kept2'/`f2', `wmask1 declined',
    `wbase1 stored'/`sg'/`wen').  THE OWED CELLS ARE THREE, and five rows close them because two cells are
    closed by one row apiece at each window:

      R1 `wmask0 filter'      MASK0 +0x140  seed 0xfff00000     write 0xffffffff000fffff  -> reads 0
      R2 `wmask1 filter-zero' MASK1 +0x160  seed 0xfff00000     write 0xffffffff000fffff  -> reads 0
      R3 `wmask1 f2'          MASK1 +0x160  seed 0x700000       write 0x200000            -> reads 0x700000
      R4 `wbase0 filter'      BASE0 +0x100  seed 0x1fff00000    write 0xfffffffc000fffff  -> reads 0
      R5 `wbase1 filter'      BASE1 +0x120  seed 0x1fff00000    write 0xfffffffc000fffff  -> reads 0
      R6 `wen silent both'    no writes: assert ZERO WEN announcements across the new session

    EACH ROW CHECKS THE PAIR (seed readback, final readback), so the seed costs no separate scored row.

    *** THE DISCRIMINATING BIT IS NAMED FOR EVERY VALUE, which is what I asked for and is what the round
    got wrong once already. ***
      * R1/R2's write is the 64-bit COMPLEMENT of the MASK store mask, so a correct build normalises to
        v = 0, f = 0, f & (f+1) = 0 and stores zero.  A widened store mask leaks the preserved bits into
        the readback (0x1000 for the bit-12 form, 0xff000 for M43); a narrowed one shows in the SEED
        readback instead; a raw-input validator (M44) declines and the seed survives.
      * R3: field 2 gives 2 & 3 = 2, so bit 1 of the predicate result is what separates `== 0' from
        `<= 2', and the stored value differs from the checked seed.
      * R4/R5's write is the complement of the BASE store mask: bits 0-19 and 34-63 set, architected bits
        20-33 clear, so a store mask that keeps ANY reserved bit shows it -- M47 retains exactly
        0x400000000.
      * AND THE OLD TRAP IS RE-CONFIRMED RATHER THAN FORGOTTEN: 0x1fff00000 >> 20 = 0x1fff and
        0x1fff & 0x2000 = 0, so that value still does NOT distinguish MASK raw-field validation.  It is
        used here only as a BASE SEED, where the field predicate is not what does the work.
    R6 kills the previously unrowed BASE1 announce cell without adding a write, because both BASE seeds and
    both subsequent writes clear WEN.

    COST: 73 scored rows in 24 sessions, against 67 rows today.  That is the figure to weigh before
    running -- this project has measured that added rows perturb other mutants' scores and force a full
    re-table, so the closure buys a re-table of every leg.


